<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bridei son of Beli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 693</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Bridei III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bridei son of Beli was King of Fortriu and overking of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> from about 671. In 685 he destroyed the Northumbrian army and killed King Ecgfrith at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain">Battle of Dun Nechtain</a>, ending English overlordship in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. He also campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and against Dunnottar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dundurn">Dundurn</a>, strengthening Pictish power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-beli.jpg" alt="Bridei son of Beli" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greenshed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pictish_Stone_at_Aberlemno_Church_Yard_-_Battle_Scene_Detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bridei was a son of Beli, king of the British kingdom of Altclut, whose death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a> place in 627, so Bridei was born no later than 628; a verse attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a> calls him son of the king of Dumbarton. Nennius' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a> makes him a maternal first cousin of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith of Northumbria</a>, which suggests his mother was a daughter of King Edwin of Deira. His grandfather Neithon son of Guipno may have been the Pictish king recorded as Nechtan grandson of Uerb.</p><p>He was probably brought up at the Northumbrian court, possibly taken there as a hostage by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiu">Oswiu</a> after his half-brother Eugein of Alt Clut killed the Dál Riatan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc">Domnall Brecc</a> in 643. From 653 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> were under Northumbrian overlordship. In 671 Drest son of Donuel, who had led a rising against it, was expelled, and Bridei's accession was probably engineered by Ecgfrith together with Pictish supporters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign Bridei intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>: he may have been involved in the killing of its king Domangart mac Domnaill in 673, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record that many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> drowned in Loch Awe in 676. After Ecgfrith's defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-of-mercia">Æthelred of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trent">Battle of the Trent</a> in 679 he built a confederation of Pictish territories that owed him allegiance and tribute, closely modelled on Northumbrian overlordship, through campaigns that reached Dunbeath in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunadd">Dunadd</a> in mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, Ecgfrith invaded because Bridei stopped paying tribute. On Saturday 20 May 685 the Northumbrians, lured into what Bede called the narrow passes of inaccessible mountains, were destroyed at Dun Nechtain. The site has generally been placed at Dunnichen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a>, though since 2006 Dunachton in Badenoch has been proposed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> in Pictish lands fled or were killed or enslaved, and Trumwine, the Northumbrian bishop of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abercorn">Abercorn</a>, withdrew to Whitby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bridei was buried on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>, and a lament for him is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adomnan">Adomnán</a>, abbot of Iona. He is the first king called rex Fortrenn, king of Fortriu, in contemporary sources, and his reign opened what historians call the Verturian hegemony, in which Fortriu grew into the overkingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. After his victory the Picts appear in documents as a single gens rather than plural gentes. The later kings Bridei and Naiton, sons of Der-Ilei, were probably his grandsons.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bridei son of Beli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 684 his army raided Brega in Ireland, and in 685 he led an invasion of Pictland that ended in disaster at the Battle of Nechtansmere, where the Pictish king <b>Bridei son of Beli</b> killed him and destroyed his army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Their greatest victory came on 20 May 685, when <b>Bridei son of Beli</b> defeated and killed the Northumbrian king Ecgfrith at Dun Nechtain, often called Nechtansmere, ending Northumbrian domination north of the Forth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Picts under King <b>Bridei son of Beli</b> defeated the Northumbrians, and Ecgfrith, who had ruled Northumbria at the height of its power, lost his life.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-20">20 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bridei son of Beli</b> destroyed the Northumbrian army at Dun Nechtain in 685, ending English dominance in the north. Óengus son of Fergus, from 729 to 761, defeated rivals and even campaigned against Dál Riata and Strathclyde.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fortriu">Kingdom of Fortriu</a></li></ul></section>
